%n būdāša | ellētu | ṣubāta | ul kuttumā
#note: See @bib{RN117@901}: “29-30. As Shaffer had already implied, the Akkadian of these lines renders the Sumerian of Inanna’s Descent 232-3 // 259-60 more nearly than that of BN 202-3. The adjective @sux{ku₃} = @i{ellu}, used of shoulders, has connotations of cclour: like other residents of the Netherworld Ereškigal is deprived of sun, and her gleaming white flesh stands out in the dark. The @sux{bur.šagan} is translated here as @i{pūr šappāti} but in l. 50 as @i{pūr šikkati}, reflecting the dual entries in lexical texts: @sux{[ša-gan dug.šagan]} = @akk{[šik]-ka-t[u]}, @akk{[šap]-pa-t[u]} @i{Diri} V 256–7 (@i{CAD} Š/1, p. 477); cf. @i{Hh} X 103-4 These equations suggest that the @sux{bur.šagan} was a vessel shaped like wine jar (@i{šappatu}) or an oil flask (@i{šikkatu}), i.e. narrow of neck and bulbous of body. It was typically made of stone (for three instances of @sux{{na₄}bur.šagan} in administrative documents see @i{PSD} B, p. 183). According to @i{Lugale} 599, where @sux{{na₄}bur.šagan} is also translated @akk{pu-u₂-ru šik-ka-tum}, this stone was @sux{{na₄}mar.hu.ša} = @i{marhušû}, commonly rendered ‘marcasite’, and the vessel was used for filtration of water, oil or wine (see @bib{RN101@220}, and my note on p. 222 of that volume). The verb at the end of l. 30 has caused difficulty in the past. The correspondence with gid in Inanna’s Descent 233 // 260 makes @i{šadādu}, ‘to pull, draw’, the obvious derivation, though von Soden read @i{naddāta} (@bib{LS304@234}: ‘ist nicht behängt’; followed by @bib{SladekPhD1974@209}). I assume that the verb matches @sux{gada bur₂} in BN 203 and so conveys not the shape of the breast or the bowl (‘drawn out’) but the drawing over the breast of a garment (so already Heidel; for the phrase @i{šidda}/@i{ṣubāta šadādu} see @i{CAD} Š/1, p. 22). Most recent translations have lost sight of the reference of the simile, which applies to the breast and not the draping. Speiser already saw the point, translating ‘her cruse-shaped breasts are not wrapped in cloth’. Ereškigal’s breasts, the clothing rent from them in mourning, are seen hanging pale and pendulous like twin flasks of marcasite.”
// (Gilgameš, Enkidu, and the Netherworld 202)
// cf. L I.4 SB "XII" 49

